1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,640
 So now let's talk about Amazon Bedrock.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:06,200
 So Amazon Bedrock is the one service on AWS.

3
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,160
 They're going to use to build generative AI applications.

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:15,240
 So it's very powerful and it allows you through an interface to play with many models

5
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:18,240
 and configure them in order to get the outcome you want.

6
00:00:18,240 --> 00:00:21,840
 So it's called a fully managed service that means that for you,

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:23,680
 there is no service into managed.

8
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:28,480
 You just use the service on AWS and Amazon Web Services will make sure

9
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:31,040
 that the service is working for you.

10
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:36,240
 So you're going to keep control of all the data you're going to use to train the model

11
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,640
 because it all happens within your account.

12
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:40,720
 It never leaves your accounts.

13
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,520
 You're going to have a paper use pricing model

14
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:46,880
 but we'll go into the pricing model later on as well.

15
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,880
 There's a unified API so that means that to access Amazon Bedrock

16
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:53,200
 and the many models behind Amazon Bedrock,

17
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,600
 you only have one way of doing it which is standardized.

18
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:59,200
 You can leverage a wide array of foundation models.

19
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:02,160
 We'll have a look at them in a second and on top of it,

20
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:06,480
 you have advanced features such as RAG or LLM agents

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:12,080
 and you get security, privacy, governance and responsible AI features as well

22
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,200
 within Amazon Bedrock.

23
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:15,520
 So we're going to spend a bit of time learning about it

24
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:17,200
 and practicing together.

25
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:23,280
 So what type of foundation models do we have access to on Amazon Bedrock?

26
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:29,760
 Well, many companies have agreements with AWS to publish their models on Bedrock.

27
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,480
 So we have AI21 Lab, we have cohere,

28
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:37,680
 we have stability AI, we have of course Amazon themselves,

29
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:42,480
 we have anthropic, meta, and of course over time,

30
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:45,520
 more foundation models and more companies behind these models

31
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:47,840
 are going to be added into Amazon Bedrock.

32
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,880
 So the way it works that whenever you use one of these models,

33
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:56,240
 Amazon Bedrock is going to make a copy of the foundation model, the FM,

34
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:58,240
 and it's going to be only available to you.

35
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,760
 And then in some cases, you're going to be able to use your own data

36
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,600
 to fine tune the model to your needs.

37
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:07,280
 Again, you need to know that none of your data

38
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:10,640
 is going to be used and sent back to one of these providers

39
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,240
 to train the foundation model.

40
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:16,800
 What you get happens all within your account and only within your accounts.

41
00:02:17,600 --> 00:02:21,600
 So it's always helpful to get a diagram to understand a service.

42
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:23,440
 So I mean one for Amazon Bedrock.

43
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:26,560
 So we have at the center of it foundation models.

44
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:29,680
 And those are the ones we've seen and we'll have a look in the hands-on

45
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:31,920
 to see how we can select them.

46
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:34,480
 And we're going to have an interactive playground

47
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,440
 that we're going to have a look at it as well in the next lecture

48
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:41,600
 in which, as a user, we're going to select a model we want to use.

49
